From e52cab215e04b0d831ade9ecd0f9b6f0a815066a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 06:08:43 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Prediction by linear extrapolation: default prediction after
 an integration failure

With the LINEAR_EXTRAPOLATION_PREDICTION strategy, a resolution failed when
the behaviours could not be integrated at the extrapolated estimate, although
they could be at the default prediction: the non linear solvers stop at once
when the integration fails at their first estimate, and the time step was
rejected.

The problem now keeps the outcome of the first integration of the behaviours
which follows the extrapolation (reported by integrate, the computations of
the prediction operators being ignored). If it failed, solve restores the
default prediction (unknowns at the beginning of the time step, values
imposed at its end), prints a warning and calls the non linear solver again,
once. The reference of the relative criterion is unchanged: it is already
the norm of the residual of the default prediction, so that the resolution
is the one of the default strategy. The failures of the integration at the
following estimates of a resolution are still handled by the non linear
solver. No integration is added when the behaviours are integrated at the
extrapolated estimate, and the resolutions of the other strategies are
unchanged.

LinearExtrapolationPredictionTest, with the FailingIsotropicLinearElasticity
behaviour and the three solvers: an imposed displacement which increases then
decreases, for which the behaviour is integrated at the default prediction of
the time step of the reversal and not at its extrapolated estimate (first
estimate equal to the default prediction, reference, iterations and solution
of the default strategy, one warning; extrapolated estimate kept with a
shorter time step; increment of this time step extrapolated at the next
one), and an imposed stress beyond the limit of the behaviour, for which the
resolution fails without starting again from the default prediction.
